WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.858
Electricity flows round a loop. Break
the loop anywhere, and it stops.

2
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:13.397
A cell pushes it round. A switch
lets you stop it when you want.

3
00:00:13.897 --> 00:00:18.495
A cell, two wires and a lamp
make one loop: a series circuit.

4
00:00:18.715 --> 00:00:22.476
The wire runs from the cell to
the lamp, and back to the cell.

5
00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:26.644
The loop is complete. Electricity
flows, and the lamp lights.

6
00:00:26.864 --> 00:00:30.718
Now a wire comes loose. That
gap is a break in the circuit.

7
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:35.629
Nothing can flow, so the lamp
goes out. One break stops it all.

8
00:00:36.129 --> 00:00:40.587
A switch is a break you control.
Inside are two metal contacts.

9
00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:45.869
Closed, the contacts touch. The loop
is complete and the lamp lights.

10
00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:51.244
Open, there is a gap between them.
Nothing flows, and the lamp is dark.

11
00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:54.900
The cell is still full. It
is the path that is broken.

12
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.625
A doorbell button is a switch. It
closes only while you press it.

13
00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:03.839
Let go, and it springs
open again. The bell stops.

14
00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:08.983
Cells push the electricity round
the loop. One cell, one lamp.

15
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:13.011
Add a second cell. The push is
bigger, so the lamp is brighter.

16
00:01:13.231 --> 00:01:17.596
Now add a second lamp. The same
push is shared between the two.

17
00:01:17.816 --> 00:01:21.578
A shared push means each lamp
is dimmer than it was before.

18
00:01:21.798 --> 00:01:27.278
More cells: brighter. More lamps:
dimmer. Open the switch: off.

19
00:01:27.778 --> 00:01:32.050
Leave a gap in the loop. Put a
material in that gap and test it.

20
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.125
A conductor lets electricity
through, so the lamp lights up.

21
00:01:36.345 --> 00:01:40.989
Copper wire, a steel key, a coin:
metals are the best conductors.

22
00:01:41.209 --> 00:01:46.503
An insulator does not let it through.
Plastic, rubber, wood, glass.

23
00:01:46.723 --> 00:01:51.878
A shiny plastic lid is an insulator.
It is the metal that conducts.

24
00:01:52.098 --> 00:01:55.906
That is why a wire is copper
inside and plastic outside.

25
00:01:56.406 --> 00:02:00.260
Build your circuit with cells
only. Never the plug socket.

26
00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.892
A cell gives a small, safe push.
Mains electricity can kill.

27
00:02:05.112 --> 00:02:09.663
Water carries electricity. Dry your
hands before you touch a switch.

28
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:13.738
If a wire gets hot, open the
switch and tell a grown-up.

29
00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:17.906
Today you add cells and lamps,
and watch the light change.

30
00:02:18.126 --> 00:02:22.306
You open and close the switch, then
test eight materials in the gap.

31
00:02:22.526 --> 00:02:26.102
Write each one in a table:
conductor, or insulator.

32
00:02:26.322 --> 00:02:31.337
And choose the right equipment: a
cell for brighter, never more wire.

33
00:02:31.837 --> 00:02:36.063
One loop, all the way round. A
break anywhere stops everything.

34
00:02:36.283 --> 00:02:41.763
A switch opens and closes a gap. More
cells, brighter. More lamps, dimmer.

35
00:02:41.983 --> 00:02:46.024
Metals conduct. Plastic,
rubber, wood and glass insulate.
